/*!
  \page messageSystemPage Status and error messages
  The DIANA framework libraries provide a common messaging system for reading
  and writing framework files. This page introduces the system purpose and its
  setup. To get more detailed insight, see the documentation of the involved
  classes.

  For the building blocks, see IModelFile, IModelFileItem and CModelFileObs.

  For the application to the DIANA data file, see CDiaDataFile, CDatInputFile
  and CDatInputField.

  For the application to the Nastran format, see CNastranFile, CNasCard and
  CNasField.

  \section msgReportingSection Message reporting
  An input/output module will most often have a hierarchy of classes. At the
  topmost level there is an IModelFile specialization. Its interface provides
  members to put messages to the outside world, for example
  IModelFile::putFatalMessage(). Specialized code calling this member must
  provide all message information, like text, code, severity and location.
  
  A more abstract way of putting messages is provided in the IModelFileItem
  class. By specializing this class, client code just needs to provide the
  message or error and the location information will be appended by
  IModelFileItem.

  Note that both classes provide the messaging functionality by non-const
  members. The reason behind it is that no processing can take place in a const
  member causing state changes and thus messages.
  
  \subsection msgTextSection Message text
  All message texts must be marked in code for translation by tr(). Wherever
  possible, use arguments for reusability of the translation.

  \subsection msgSeveritySection Error severity
  There are four types of messages identified.
  -# A status message for passing information not related to unexpected
     situations.
  -# A warning message for situations that might cause an undesired result, but
     do not invalidate the process as such.
  -# A fatal message for situations that invalidate the process result.
  -# An abort message for situations that not only invalidate the process
     result, but also force the immediate abortion of the process.

  \subsection msgCodeSection Error codes
  The primary purpose of error codes is to facilitate quality feedback to users
  encountering errors and to aid maintenance. All warning, fatal and abort
  messages processed by the framework need a distinct error number.

  The error numbers are grouped by module using ranges of numbers. For instance
  the NastranFile library has error numbers in the range of 5000-6999.
  Within the library the grouping is refined to classes, using a convenient
  increase (100). For example one class uses error numbers starting with 5301,
  the next class starts numbering at 5401.

  The CFilosModelFile redirects errors from the filos kernel code. The error
  numbers already in use are augmented with 9000 to get into the range
  9000-9999.

  The programmer is responsible for staying within a specific range and for
  avoiding duplicate error numbers. For this he/she can use the script command
  \a gpm, which stands for 'get put messages'.
  This will provide a sorted list of all error messaging calls in the code.
  It makes it very easy to identify ranges of numbers and spot numbers not in
  use. Note that the 9000- range, originating from the filos kernel code, does
  not show up in the list.
  
  Needless to say, error numbers should not change once in use. It is a unique
  identification used by quality assurance, support, users and possibly in
  documentation.

  \subsection msgLocationSection Error location
  Part of the feedback about an error is about the location in the file. When
  messaging at the IModelFile level, one must provide the information of a
  TFilePosition explicitly. Examples are line number and character position.

  When messaging at the IModelFileItem level, this information is automatically
  retreived and passed. The functionality is in operator format so that
  IModelFileItem specializations can pass for a TFilePosition argument.
  
  \section msgExceptionSection Exception handling
  The framework will throw an exception after a fatal or abort message has
  been put to the world outside. By this mechanism the client code can recover
  from errors at the appropriate level. For instance a fatal message for
  reading some coordinate could be dealt with at line level by proceeding to
  the next coordinate. The programmer needs to catch the exception at the level
  at which the processing needs to continue. The idea is to validate as much
  of the input as is possible.

  \subsection msgExceptionTypesSection Exception types
  The standard type of exceptions in the input/output framework are
  TAbortException and TFatalException. The implementation of an input/output
  module must catch these at the appropriate level.
  
  The TAbortException is meant to abort the entire process. Therefore it needs
  to be catched at the toplevel entry.

  The TFatalException is meant to invalidate the process, but to continue for
  collecting as much validation information as possible. It needs to be catched
  at least at the toplevel but rather at multiple levels.

  \subsection msgThrowCatchSection About throw try catch
  The exceptions are thrown allocated on the stack. They need to be catched by
  reference or type.

  \todo Entry and reference to style about throwing on heap versus stack.

  \section msgUsersSection Messaging users
  The framework brings the messages to the toplevel object, which in turn
  emits the messages to the outside world.
  
  To pass these messages to end users, an observer class is needed. This class
  needs some slots with the signals connected to them.
  In the slots custom formatting can take place.
  
  A simple example, used for testing, is CModelFileObs. It is
  very straightforward to create a similar class, showing the messages in
  a dialog.

  \todo Add progress messaging (and cancelling?)
*/
